Approach 2 — Recursive DFS

This is the cleanest solution.

Idea

At each node:

If null → valid.
If leaf → valid.
Compute sum of existing children.
If current value differs → false.
Recursively validate left and right subtrees.

The single-child case becomes very simple if we treat a missing child as contributing 0.

So:

sum = 0

if left exists:
    sum += left.data

if right exists:
    sum += right.data

Then:

root.data == sum
Code
class Solution {

    public boolean isSumProperty(Node root) {

        if (root == null) {
            return true;
        }

        // Leaf node
        if (root.left == null && root.right == null) {
            return true;
        }

        int sum = 0;

        if (root.left != null) {
            sum += root.left.data;
        }

        if (root.right != null) {
            sum += root.right.data;
        }

        // Current node violates the property
        if (root.data != sum) {
            return false;
        }

        // Check both subtrees
        return isSumProperty(root.left)
                && isSumProperty(root.right);
    }
}

