Every node is visited at most once:

Time = O(n)

Recursive call stack:

Space = O(h)

where h is tree height.

Worst case:

O(n)

Balanced tree:

O(log n)

This is the best standard recursive solution.

Approach 3 — Iterative DFS Using Stack

We can remove recursion and use an explicit stack.

The logic is exactly the same.

For every node popped from the stack:

leaf? → skip
otherwise calculate child sum
mismatch? → false
push children
Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public boolean isSumProperty(Node root) {

        if (root == null) {
            return true;
        }

        Stack<Node> stack = new Stack<>();

        stack.push(root);

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {

            Node curr = stack.pop();

            // Leaf node
            if (curr.left == null && curr.right == null) {
                continue;
            }

            int sum = 0;

            if (curr.left != null) {
                sum += curr.left.data;
            }

            if (curr.right != null) {
                sum += curr.right.data;
            }

            if (curr.data != sum) {
                return false;
            }

            if (curr.right != null) {
                stack.push(curr.right);
            }

            if (curr.left != null) {
                stack.push(curr.left);
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}

